course: world
lesson: 9
title: Time zones and the calendar
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Because the Earth turns, noon comes to different places at different times: when it is noon in London, it is already evening in China and still early morning in New York.
explain: So the world is divided into time zones, roughly 24 of them, one for each hour of the Earth's turn; going east, the clocks are usually one hour later in each new zone.
explain: Our calendar follows the sky: a day is one turn of the Earth, a month comes from the Moon's phases, and a year is one trip of the Earth around the Sun.
explain: A year is not exactly 365 days but about 365 days and 6 hours, so every four years the four extra quarter days add up to one whole day, and we add 29 February: that is a leap year.
explain: Without leap years the calendar would slip by about one day every four years, and after about 750 years the summer months would fall in the middle of winter.
fact: Because the Earth turns, it is noon at different times in different places.
fact: The world is divided into time zones.
fact: Going east, the clock usually moves one hour later in each new time zone.
fact: There are 24 hours in a day, so there are roughly 24 main time zones.
fact: The Sun rises earlier in places farther east.
fact: China and Japan lie far to the east of Ukraine and France, and New York lies far to the west of London, across the ocean.
fact: A year is about 365 days and 6 hours long.
fact: A leap year has 366 days, with an extra day on 29 February.
fact: Most years divisible by 4 are leap years, such as 2024 and 2028.
fact: Years ending in 00 are leap years only if they divide by 400, so 2000 was a leap year but 1900 was not.
fact: A month comes from the Moon, which goes from full moon to full moon in about 29 and a half days.
fact: A day comes from one turn of the Earth, and a year from one trip around the Sun.
fact: Without leap years, the seasons would slowly move to different months.
exercise: Why is it not the same time everywhere on the Earth? | The Earth turns, so the Sun is high over different places at different times | Different countries choose to set their clocks however they like, just for fun | The Sun moves faster over some countries | Some countries are closer to the Sun
exercise: What happens to the clock as you travel east into a new time zone? | It usually moves one hour later | It usually moves one hour earlier | It stays exactly the same | It moves one day later
exercise: About how many main time zones are there? | About 24 | About 12 | About 7 | About 365
exercise: Where does the Sun rise first, in China or in France? | In China, because it is farther east | In France, because it is farther west | In both at the same moment | In France, because it is farther north
exercise: How long is a year really? | About 365 days and 6 hours | Exactly 365 days, no more and no less | Exactly 360 days, twelve months of 30 | About 366 and a half days long
exercise: Why do we have leap years? | To add up the extra quarter days of each year | To give February the same length as March | Because the Earth spins slower every four years | Because the Moon is late every four years
exercise: How many days are in a leap year? | 366 | 365 | 364 | 367
exercise: Which date is added in a leap year? | 29 February | 31 February | 30 February | 31 April
exercise: Which of these years was a leap year? | 2000 | 1900 | 2001 | 1999
exercise: Which part of the calendar comes from the Moon? | The month | The length of a day | The length of a year | The sixty-minute hour
exercise: What is one year? | One trip of the Earth around the Sun | One turn of the Earth around its axis | One trip of the Moon around the Earth | One trip of the Sun around the Earth
exercise: What would happen with no leap years over hundreds of years? | The seasons would move to different months | The year would get a little shorter each time | The days would slowly get longer and longer | The Moon would stop having its phases each month
exercise: Why was 1900 not a leap year? | Years ending in 00 must divide by 400 | It did not divide by 4 | Leap years started after 1950 | A war stopped the calendar that year
exercise: Is 2028 a leap year? | Yes, it divides by 4 | No, it ends in 8 | No, only even hundreds are leap years | Yes, every year after 2024 is
test: It is 12 noon in London. Which is most likely true? | It is evening in Beijing and early morning in New York | It is noon everywhere on the Earth | It is evening in New York and morning in Beijing | It is midnight in Paris
test: A plane flies west across three time zones. How should the passengers set their watches? | Back by about three hours | Forward by about three hours | Forward by one day | They need not change them
test: Olga was born on 29 February. How often is her real birthday date on the calendar? | Usually once every four years | Every single year, like everyone else | Only once every ten years or so | Never again after the day she was born
test: If the year were exactly 365 days long, what would we do with leap years? | We would not need them | We would need two each year | We would need one every year | We would need one every hundred years
test: Which is true? | The day comes from the Earth's turn and the year from its trip around the Sun | The day comes from the Moon and the year from the Earth's turn on its own axis | The month comes from the Sun and the year from the Moon | The day and the year both come from the Moon
test: A town in the far east of a country sees sunrise before a town in the west. Why? | The Earth turns towards the east, so the east meets the Sun first | The eastern town is closer to the Sun | The Sun is bigger in the east | The western town has more clouds
test: Will the year 2100 be a leap year? | No, because it ends in 00 and does not divide by 400 | Yes, because it divides by 4 | Yes, because every year ending in 00 is | No, because leap years will end in 2050
test: A calendar with no leap years has been used for 400 years. What has happened? | It has slipped about a hundred days behind the seasons | It is still exactly right | It has slipped about one day behind | It has slipped a whole year behind
test: Why can a phone call from Ukraine to Japan wake someone up? | It may already be night there because Japan is far to the east | Japan's clocks always run slower, so they are hours behind Ukraine | Japan has longer days than Ukraine | The Sun never sets in Japan
test: its 9 in the morning in london, is it 9 in new york too | No, New York is a few hours earlier | Yes, the clock is the same everywhere | No, New York is a few hours later | No, New York is a whole day ahead
test: how come feb's got 29 days some years | To keep the calendar in step with the extra 6 hours each year | Because the Moon goes faster those years | Because the Earth spins slower in February | Just to make February longer for fun
test: Mia says 1900 was a leap year cos it divides by 4. true? | No, years ending in 00 need to divide by 400 | Yes, every year that divides by 4 is a leap year | Yes, and so was every year ending in 00 | No, leap years never end in 0
test: Leo says the Sun comes up at the same time all over the world. right? | No, it rises earlier in places farther east | Yes, sunrise is at the same moment everywhere | Yes, but only on the equator | No, it rises earlier in places farther west
exam: Identify the principal reason for the division of the world into time zones. | The rotation of the Earth brings noon to different places at different times | The Sun shines with differing intensity upon different nations | The calendar differs between the continents | The Moon is visible at different hours in different places
exam: State the number of days comprised in a leap year. | Three hundred and sixty-six | Three hundred and sixty-five | Three hundred and sixty-four | Three hundred and sixty
exam: It is given that years ending in two zeros are leap years only if divisible by four hundred. Ascertain which of the following was a leap year. | The year 2000 | The year 1900 | The year 1800 | The year 2100
exam: Whereas a traveller proceeds eastward into the adjacent time zone, determine the customary adjustment of the clock. | It is advanced by one hour | It is put back by one hour | It is advanced by twelve hours | It remains unaltered
